<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alexandra Feodorovna (Alix of Hesse)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1872–1918</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Alix of Hesse, Empress Alexandra of Russia, Empress Alexandra Feodorovna</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alexandra Feodorovna (1872–1918), born Princess Alix of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> and by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, was the last Empress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> as wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicholas-ii">Nicholas II</a>. A granddaughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/queen-victoria">Queen Victoria</a>, she married Nicholas in 1894. Her reliance on the mystic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grigori-rasputin">Grigori Rasputin</a>, who seemed to ease her son Alexei's haemophilia, damaged the monarchy during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. She was killed with her family by the Bolsheviks at Yekaterinburg in July 1918.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexandra-feodorovna-alix-of-hesse.jpg" alt="Alexandra Feodorovna (Alix of Hesse)" width="320" height="425" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Boasson and Eggler St. Petersburg Nevsky 24.Bain News Service, publisher · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alexandra_Fyodorovna_LOC_01137u.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
